1
00:00:04,700 --> 00:00:06,310
Welcome to Helm From Zero.

2
00:00:06,310 --> 00:00:10,024
We start with an application you already know how to deploy:

3
00:00:10,024 --> 00:00:13,924
five services and a database, written as plain Kubernetes YAML,

4
00:00:13,924 --> 00:00:17,577
copied three times for development, staging and production.

5
00:00:18,133 --> 00:00:21,119
Then we replace those copies with one Helm chart:

6
00:00:21,119 --> 00:00:25,020
templates written once, and a small values file per environment.

7
00:00:25,567 --> 00:00:28,640
And we operate it the way teams do every week:

8
00:00:28,640 --> 00:00:34,386
install, upgrade, test, and roll back, with a release history that records everything.

9
00:00:34,933 --> 00:00:39,583
Twenty levels, from the YAML problem to a production-style chart and a capstone.

10
00:00:39,583 --> 00:00:44,872
Along the way we break things on purpose, and investigate them like we would in production.

11
00:00:46,633 --> 00:00:49,774
Everything comes from one free repository on GitHub.

12
00:00:49,774 --> 00:00:55,452
Each lesson is a Markdown file whose commands run automatically, on a real Kubernetes cluster.

13
00:00:55,452 --> 00:00:58,955
Every terminal in this video is a recording of those runs.

14
00:00:59,500 --> 00:01:05,053
Seventeen labs, each with a part where you break something and a part where you investigate it,

15
00:01:05,053 --> 00:01:09,320
then twelve troubleshooting scenarios, twelve challenges, and a capstone.

16
00:01:11,067 --> 00:01:12,550
The lab cluster is kind:

17
00:01:12,550 --> 00:01:17,556
Kubernetes one point thirty-seven, each node a Docker container on your computer.

18
00:01:18,100 --> 00:01:19,850
Traefik routes the traffic.

19
00:01:19,850 --> 00:01:23,220
Nothing is deployed yet, so it answers four oh four:

20
00:01:23,220 --> 00:01:25,100
proof that requests reach it.

21
00:01:25,100 --> 00:01:25,813
And notice:

22
00:01:25,813 --> 00:01:29,118
Traefik itself was installed with one Helm command.

23
00:01:29,118 --> 00:01:33,072
By chapter sixteen you will be able to read every word of it.

24
00:01:34,833 --> 00:01:37,372
Helm is the package manager for Kubernetes.

25
00:01:37,372 --> 00:01:40,265
A chart packages the templates of an application,

26
00:01:40,265 --> 00:01:46,583
with a Chart.yaml that gives it a name and a version, and a values.yaml with every setting and its default.

27
00:01:47,133 --> 00:01:51,318
Each environment adds a small file with only what differs:

28
00:01:51,318 --> 00:01:54,565
replicas, host names, resources, autoscaling.

29
00:01:55,100 --> 00:01:59,285
Helm renders the templates with those values into plain Kubernetes YAML,

30
00:01:59,285 --> 00:02:02,425
on your computer, before Kubernetes receives anything.

31
00:02:02,967 --> 00:02:09,328
And it records each installation as a release, with numbered revisions you can inspect, upgrade and roll back.

32
00:02:11,067 --> 00:02:13,773
One distinction to get right from the start.

33
00:02:13,773 --> 00:02:16,233
The chart version describes the package.

34
00:02:16,767 --> 00:02:19,510
The app version describes the software inside:

35
00:02:19,510 --> 00:02:22,491
usually the image tag that is deployed by default.

36
00:02:23,033 --> 00:02:26,948
Add an option to the chart, and only the chart version moves.

37
00:02:27,500 --> 00:02:31,124
Ship a new application release, and the app version moves,

38
00:02:31,124 --> 00:02:35,247
and the chart version too, because the package's contents changed.

39
00:02:35,247 --> 00:02:37,746
A published chart version never changes.

40
00:02:39,500 --> 00:02:42,120
Before Helm, let's see the problem.

41
00:02:42,120 --> 00:02:45,863
Here is the Bookshop, deployed with kubectl apply:

42
00:02:45,863 --> 00:02:49,906
five Deployments, a database, Services and an Ingress.

43
00:02:49,906 --> 00:02:50,804
All running.

44
00:02:51,367 --> 00:02:57,007
The frontend reports environment dev, and the users come back from the database.

45
00:02:57,007 --> 00:02:58,205
Plain YAML works.

46
00:02:58,205 --> 00:03:01,167
For one environment, it is perfectly good.

47
00:03:02,900 --> 00:03:06,136
Now imagine maintaining it across three environments.

48
00:03:06,136 --> 00:03:11,936
About four hundred sixty lines each, and the diff between dev and staging is a few dozen lines:

49
00:03:11,936 --> 00:03:13,096
the rest is copies.

50
00:03:13,633 --> 00:03:16,751
The node A P I team releases a new version.

51
00:03:16,751 --> 00:03:18,926
Where do you change the image?

52
00:03:18,926 --> 00:03:20,014
In three files.

53
00:03:20,014 --> 00:03:24,001
Forget one, and your environments silently drift apart.

54
00:03:24,567 --> 00:03:29,181
And to make the folder applicable as-is, the password is written in a file,

55
00:03:29,181 --> 00:03:32,688
in Git, readable by everyone who can read the repository.

56
00:03:32,688 --> 00:03:34,841
That is the problem Helm addresses.

57
00:03:36,600 --> 00:03:38,826
Install Helm with an official method:

58
00:03:38,826 --> 00:03:42,857
the get helm four script, Homebrew, winget, or the release archive.

59
00:03:42,857 --> 00:03:45,804
This course is tested with Helm four point three.

60
00:03:46,367 --> 00:03:47,748
Helm has no server.

61
00:03:47,748 --> 00:03:52,475
It uses your kubeconfig, like kubectl, and sees the same cluster:

62
00:03:52,475 --> 00:03:55,383
here the Traefik release from the setup.

63
00:03:55,933 --> 00:03:59,405
Helm's own files hold the repositories you add and a cache.

64
00:03:59,405 --> 00:04:00,935
The releases are not here:

65
00:04:00,935 --> 00:04:05,171
they live in the cluster, which is why your teammates see the same list.

66
00:04:06,933 --> 00:04:09,193
Now let's intentionally break it.

67
00:04:09,193 --> 00:04:13,303
A typo in the context name, and Helm cannot reach a cluster.

68
00:04:13,867 --> 00:04:18,418
The error names what it looked for; the list of contexts shows what exists.

69
00:04:18,418 --> 00:04:20,602
One more check worth making a habit:

70
00:04:20,602 --> 00:04:21,391
helm version.

71
00:04:21,391 --> 00:04:24,789
With two copies of Helm on the PATH, the first one wins,

72
00:04:24,789 --> 00:04:30,493
and while this course was being built, an older one made every hook wait for its full timeout.

73
00:04:32,233 --> 00:04:35,763
helm create generates a complete, conventional chart:

74
00:04:35,763 --> 00:04:39,492
Chart.yaml, values.yaml, and templates for a Deployment,

75
00:04:39,492 --> 00:04:45,085
a Service, an Ingress, an autoscaler, a service account, helpers, NOTES, and a test.

76
00:04:45,085 --> 00:04:47,949
Don't delete what you don't understand yet:

77
00:04:47,949 --> 00:04:51,745
this skeleton is what most charts in the world look like.

78
00:04:52,300 --> 00:04:54,816
Install it, and you have a release:

79
00:04:54,816 --> 00:04:57,835
name hello, revision one, status deployed.

80
00:04:57,835 --> 00:05:00,351
The NOTES come from a template too.

81
00:05:00,900 --> 00:05:03,384
helm test runs the chart's test Pod:

82
00:05:03,384 --> 00:05:06,489
it calls the Service from inside the cluster.

83
00:05:06,489 --> 00:05:07,180
Succeeded.

84
00:05:08,933 --> 00:05:11,112
Now let's intentionally break it:

85
00:05:11,112 --> 00:05:12,630
a chart without a name.

86
00:05:12,630 --> 00:05:17,713
helm lint reports it, and exits with an error, so a pipeline would stop here.

87
00:05:18,267 --> 00:05:19,179
The challenge:

88
00:05:19,179 --> 00:05:25,243
deploy our application instead of nginx, the Bookshop frontend on port eight thousand eighty,

89
00:05:25,243 --> 00:05:27,590
with values only, no template edits.

90
00:05:27,590 --> 00:05:30,068
The test passes against the new image.

91
00:05:31,833 --> 00:05:35,992
charts slash demo app is the Bookshop frontend packaged with Helm.

92
00:05:35,992 --> 00:05:41,033
Most of the time you will install charts other people wrote, so read them first:

93
00:05:41,033 --> 00:05:44,310
helm show chart, helm show values, helm show readme.

94
00:05:44,310 --> 00:05:46,641
The values are the chart's interface:

95
00:05:46,641 --> 00:05:49,288
every setting, its default, and a comment.

96
00:05:49,833 --> 00:05:52,388
The helpers file defines named templates:

97
00:05:52,388 --> 00:05:55,753
the full name, and the labels every object will carry.

98
00:05:57,500 --> 00:06:00,256
What changed compared with a fresh scaffold?

99
00:06:00,256 --> 00:06:03,263
A checksum of the ConfigMap as a Pod annotation:

100
00:06:03,263 --> 00:06:06,521
change the configuration, and the Pods are replaced.

101
00:06:06,521 --> 00:06:08,150
A separate container port.

102
00:06:08,150 --> 00:06:12,347
And the ConfigMap passed to the container as environment variables.

103
00:06:12,347 --> 00:06:15,292
Every change has a reason you can say out loud.

104
00:06:17,033 --> 00:06:17,834
Chart.yaml.

105
00:06:17,834 --> 00:06:22,275
apiVersion v two is the chart format, not a Kubernetes A P I.

106
00:06:22,275 --> 00:06:26,570
The name becomes the default for resource names and labels.

107
00:06:26,570 --> 00:06:29,118
Type application means installable.

108
00:06:29,667 --> 00:06:33,286
version is the chart's own version, semantic versioning.

109
00:06:33,833 --> 00:06:37,297
appVersion is the application it deploys by default:

110
00:06:37,297 --> 00:06:40,028
the image tag, unless values override it.

111
00:06:41,767 --> 00:06:45,499
helm package with dash dash version and dash dash app version:

112
00:06:45,499 --> 00:06:49,654
chart one point zero point one, application one point one point zero,

113
00:06:49,654 --> 00:06:52,484
and the rendered image follows the app version.

114
00:06:52,484 --> 00:06:54,711
That is how pipelines release charts.

115
00:06:55,267 --> 00:06:58,513
Now an unquoted app version, one point ten.

116
00:06:58,513 --> 00:07:00,476
YAML reads it as a number.

117
00:07:01,033 --> 00:07:01,854
One point one:

118
00:07:01,854 --> 00:07:04,433
the image tag that would have been deployed.

119
00:07:04,433 --> 00:07:07,715
Quote anything that looks like a number but is a string.

120
00:07:09,467 --> 00:07:12,642
Before Helm, every value is fixed in the file:

121
00:07:12,642 --> 00:07:15,748
two replicas, one image tag, environment dev.

122
00:07:15,748 --> 00:07:18,233
Production means a copy of the file.

123
00:07:18,767 --> 00:07:23,265
With a template, replicas comes from dot Values dot replica count.

124
00:07:23,265 --> 00:07:25,377
Production is no longer a copy:

125
00:07:25,377 --> 00:07:27,013
it is a different value.

126
00:07:28,767 --> 00:07:30,847
Values come from four places:

127
00:07:30,847 --> 00:07:34,864
the chart's values.yaml, then each dash f file in order,

128
00:07:34,864 --> 00:07:39,885
the later one winning, then dash dash set, which wins over everything.

129
00:07:39,885 --> 00:07:41,965
Log level is set three times:

130
00:07:41,965 --> 00:07:43,257
info, warn, error.

131
00:07:43,257 --> 00:07:44,619
The last file wins.

132
00:07:44,619 --> 00:07:49,354
Replica count is three in the prod file, five on the command line:

133
00:07:49,354 --> 00:07:49,712
five.

134
00:07:49,712 --> 00:07:51,290
And features is a map:

135
00:07:51,290 --> 00:07:56,025
maps are merged key by key, so reviews survived from the defaults.

136
00:07:57,767 --> 00:08:01,360
A teammate writes replica count with a lower case c.

137
00:08:01,360 --> 00:08:02,741
No error, no effect:

138
00:08:02,741 --> 00:08:03,985
still one replica.

139
00:08:03,985 --> 00:08:09,236
Helm passes any key to the templates; only the keys a template reads matter.

140
00:08:09,800 --> 00:08:12,488
A values schema turns that into an error.

141
00:08:12,488 --> 00:08:18,325
With a minimum of one, replica count zero is rejected at lint, template and install time.

142
00:08:20,067 --> 00:08:23,509
Templates are Kubernetes YAML with placeholders.

143
00:08:23,509 --> 00:08:26,736
Double curly braces, dot Values for settings.

144
00:08:27,300 --> 00:08:28,442
Pipes and functions:

145
00:08:28,442 --> 00:08:35,351
default falls back to the app version when the tag is empty; quote makes sure an environment variable is always a string.

146
00:08:35,900 --> 00:08:39,596
Dot Release tells the template about this installation:

147
00:08:39,596 --> 00:08:42,150
its name, its namespace, its revision.

148
00:08:42,700 --> 00:08:43,528
And if, else:

149
00:08:43,528 --> 00:08:48,748
production always pulls the image, the other environments only when it is missing.

150
00:08:50,500 --> 00:08:51,775
Environment prod:

151
00:08:51,775 --> 00:08:55,527
the else branch is gone, image pull policy Always.

152
00:08:56,067 --> 00:08:57,150
range over a map:

153
00:08:57,150 --> 00:09:01,545
add a key in values, get a line in the ConfigMap, no template change.

154
00:09:01,545 --> 00:09:06,577
And quote turned the number three hundred into the string a ConfigMap requires.

155
00:09:07,133 --> 00:09:08,226
Installed for real:

156
00:09:08,226 --> 00:09:10,582
the application received the environment,

157
00:09:10,582 --> 00:09:14,606
and every Pod carries the environment and team labels from one helper.

158
00:09:16,367 --> 00:09:17,783
Two classic mistakes.

159
00:09:17,783 --> 00:09:18,862
A forgotten end:

160
00:09:18,862 --> 00:09:24,326
unexpected E O F, the parser reached the end of the file with a block still open.

161
00:09:24,867 --> 00:09:26,086
The dangerous one:

162
00:09:26,086 --> 00:09:28,661
an indentation of two instead of four.

163
00:09:28,661 --> 00:09:29,813
helm lint passes.

164
00:09:29,813 --> 00:09:30,897
Rendering works.

165
00:09:30,897 --> 00:09:33,405
The A P I server accepts the dry run.

166
00:09:33,405 --> 00:09:34,014
But look:

167
00:09:34,014 --> 00:09:36,589
the labels are no longer under labels.

168
00:09:36,589 --> 00:09:43,230
They are unknown fields directly under metadata, and the Deployment would be created without them.

169
00:09:43,230 --> 00:09:48,787
Only reading the output, or a strict schema check like kube conform, catches this.

170
00:09:50,533 --> 00:09:55,554
Helm is rendering templates into normal Kubernetes YAML before Kubernetes receives them.

171
00:09:55,554 --> 00:09:58,350
Each object comes with the template it came from.

172
00:09:58,900 --> 00:10:01,087
helm template runs without a cluster.

173
00:10:01,087 --> 00:10:06,879
The bookshop chart reads its database password back from the cluster with lookup; with no cluster,

174
00:10:06,879 --> 00:10:08,770
each render generates a new one.

175
00:10:08,770 --> 00:10:13,971
Expected here, and the reason you never apply helm template output to upgrade a release.

176
00:10:14,533 --> 00:10:21,061
dash dash dry run equals server renders with the real cluster and lets the A P I server validate every object,

177
00:10:21,061 --> 00:10:22,603
without creating anything:

178
00:10:22,603 --> 00:10:23,553
pending install.

179
00:10:25,300 --> 00:10:27,274
A chart with three mistakes:

180
00:10:27,274 --> 00:10:33,056
a tab in a values file, an invalid chart version, and an unclosed template action.

181
00:10:33,056 --> 00:10:34,254
Lint reports one:

182
00:10:34,254 --> 00:10:36,370
values files are parsed first.

183
00:10:36,933 --> 00:10:38,653
Fix it, run again:

184
00:10:38,653 --> 00:10:40,085
now Chart.yaml.

185
00:10:40,085 --> 00:10:42,378
Latest is not a version.

186
00:10:42,933 --> 00:10:43,459
Fix it:

187
00:10:43,459 --> 00:10:46,835
now the template, with the file and the line.

188
00:10:47,400 --> 00:10:47,930
Fix it:

189
00:10:47,930 --> 00:10:49,370
zero charts failed.

190
00:10:49,370 --> 00:10:51,718
Read, fix one thing, run again.

191
00:10:51,718 --> 00:10:53,537
That loop is the method.

192
00:10:53,537 --> 00:11:00,052
And lint every environment's values, because a mistake may exist only in production's.

193
00:11:01,800 --> 00:11:09,228
Install demo app as release demo, in namespace demo dev, with the dev values, and wait until it is ready.

194
00:11:09,228 --> 00:11:11,916
Revision one, deployed, and the NOTES:

195
00:11:11,916 --> 00:11:15,100
what runs, where to open it, how to check it.

196
00:11:15,633 --> 00:11:18,089
Every Pod carries the chart's labels:

197
00:11:18,089 --> 00:11:21,275
which release, which version, which environment.

198
00:11:23,033 --> 00:11:24,196
helm get values:

199
00:11:24,196 --> 00:11:26,813
exactly what this release was given.

200
00:11:27,367 --> 00:11:28,824
helm get metadata:

201
00:11:28,824 --> 00:11:34,004
chart, versions, revision, status, and Helm four's apply method:

202
00:11:34,004 --> 00:11:35,461
server side apply.

203
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:39,076
And the release itself is a Secret in the namespace,

204
00:11:39,076 --> 00:11:43,453
one per revision, holding the chart, the values and the rendered manifest.

205
00:11:43,453 --> 00:11:48,895
That is why your teammates see the same releases, and why rollback can restore any revision.

206
00:11:50,633 --> 00:11:54,438
helm uninstall removes every object of the release and its history.

207
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:55,504
Verify:

208
00:11:55,504 --> 00:11:58,238
no release, no objects with its label.

209
00:11:58,238 --> 00:12:00,612
But the namespace is still there:

210
00:12:00,612 --> 00:12:02,987
it was never part of the release.

211
00:12:02,987 --> 00:12:08,167
Volumes of StatefulSets, objects marked keep, and hook objects stay too.

212
00:12:08,167 --> 00:12:12,485
After an uninstall, check the namespace, not just helm list.

213
00:12:14,233 --> 00:12:19,751
Before upgrading, render the new state and let the A P I server compare it with what runs.

214
00:12:19,751 --> 00:12:20,609
Three changes:

215
00:12:20,609 --> 00:12:23,429
two replicas, the admin link in the ConfigMap,

216
00:12:23,429 --> 00:12:30,112
and the checksum annotation, which tells you the Pods will be replaced so they pick up the new configuration.

217
00:12:30,112 --> 00:12:32,503
Review the result, not the values file.

218
00:12:34,267 --> 00:12:38,456
Helm updates the Deployment; Kubernetes does the rolling update:

219
00:12:38,456 --> 00:12:41,794
a new ReplicaSet, new Pods ready, old Pods removed.

220
00:12:41,794 --> 00:12:44,936
Two Deployment revisions on the Kubernetes side.

221
00:12:45,500 --> 00:12:48,382
And on the Helm side, the release history:

222
00:12:48,382 --> 00:12:50,646
revision three, upgrade complete.

223
00:12:50,646 --> 00:12:54,626
Helm manages the release; Kubernetes manages the workload.

224
00:12:56,367 --> 00:12:59,792
A colleague just wants three replicas, quickly:

225
00:12:59,792 --> 00:13:02,561
helm upgrade, set replica count three.

226
00:13:02,561 --> 00:13:03,217
Deployed.

227
00:13:03,217 --> 00:13:06,496
And the application is gone from its address.

228
00:13:07,033 --> 00:13:10,215
Let's investigate this like we would in production.

229
00:13:10,215 --> 00:13:13,709
The latest revision's values contain only replica count.

230
00:13:13,709 --> 00:13:18,576
The dev file was not passed, so the release fell back to the chart's defaults:

231
00:13:18,576 --> 00:13:20,447
no Ingress, environment local.

232
00:13:20,447 --> 00:13:23,567
Helm upgrade uses only the values in this command.

233
00:13:23,567 --> 00:13:26,811
The values files are part of the deployment command:

234
00:13:26,811 --> 00:13:29,619
keep it in a script, not in someone's memory.

235
00:13:31,367 --> 00:13:32,321
Revision three:

236
00:13:32,321 --> 00:13:34,740
an image tag that was never published.

237
00:13:34,740 --> 00:13:38,050
With dash dash wait, Helm watched for sixty seconds,

238
00:13:38,050 --> 00:13:42,441
the Deployment never became ready, and the revision is marked failed.

239
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:44,527
On the Kubernetes side:

240
00:13:44,527 --> 00:13:46,785
the new Pod cannot pull its image.

241
00:13:46,785 --> 00:13:48,711
The old Pod is still running.

242
00:13:49,267 --> 00:13:51,321
And the application still answers.

243
00:13:51,321 --> 00:13:55,552
The rolling update never removes an old Pod before a new one is ready.

244
00:13:55,552 --> 00:13:59,843
Kubernetes protected the users; the release is still in a failed state.

245
00:14:01,600 --> 00:14:04,263
Back to the last good revision, two:

246
00:14:04,263 --> 00:14:05,965
rollback was a success.

247
00:14:06,500 --> 00:14:08,412
History is never rewritten.

248
00:14:08,412 --> 00:14:12,732
The rollback is a new revision, four, a copy of revision two.

249
00:14:12,732 --> 00:14:17,123
The record shows exactly what happened, including the failure.

250
00:14:17,123 --> 00:14:18,044
And remember:

251
00:14:18,044 --> 00:14:21,515
a rollback restores objects and values, not data.

252
00:14:23,267 --> 00:14:26,347
dash dash rollback on failure does it automatically:

253
00:14:26,347 --> 00:14:30,611
the upgrade fails, Helm rolls back to the previous revision immediately.

254
00:14:31,167 --> 00:14:33,627
And the one you will meet in real life:

255
00:14:33,627 --> 00:14:36,908
a deployment killed half-way, by a runner that died.

256
00:14:36,908 --> 00:14:40,379
The next upgrade says another operation is in progress.

257
00:14:40,379 --> 00:14:41,010
It is not:

258
00:14:41,010 --> 00:14:43,281
the process that started it is dead.

259
00:14:43,833 --> 00:14:49,870
Helm wrote the revision as pending upgrade before applying, and only that process could have completed it.

260
00:14:49,870 --> 00:14:52,832
The way out is a rollback to the last good revision.

261
00:14:52,832 --> 00:14:55,338
Check that nobody is really deploying first.

262
00:14:57,100 --> 00:15:02,906
One chart, three environment files, each with only what differs from the defaults.

263
00:15:02,906 --> 00:15:04,038
Dev and staging:

264
00:15:04,038 --> 00:15:05,171
nine lines each.

265
00:15:05,733 --> 00:15:08,186
Production pins the image tag explicitly,

266
00:15:08,186 --> 00:15:14,528
replaces the fixed replica count with an autoscaler between three and six, and gets more C P U and memory.

267
00:15:16,267 --> 00:15:21,063
helm upgrade dash dash install, the same command for every environment.

268
00:15:21,063 --> 00:15:26,873
Dev with one replica, staging with two, production with three, kept by the autoscaler.

269
00:15:27,433 --> 00:15:30,525
Each environment answers with its own configuration.

270
00:15:31,067 --> 00:15:35,268
And the differences between staging and production are exactly the values:

271
00:15:35,268 --> 00:15:36,006
nothing else.

272
00:15:36,006 --> 00:15:38,617
Every environment runs the same chart version,

273
00:15:38,617 --> 00:15:42,478
so a change is tested with the templates that will reach production.

274
00:15:44,233 --> 00:15:47,021
Charts are distributed by name and version.

275
00:15:47,021 --> 00:15:49,873
The author packages a chart into an archive.

276
00:15:50,433 --> 00:15:55,654
Either an H T T P repository, which is a web server with an index and the archives,

277
00:15:55,654 --> 00:16:00,498
or an O C I registry, the same kind of registry that stores container images.

278
00:16:01,033 --> 00:16:03,586
You consume them with a pinned version:

279
00:16:03,586 --> 00:16:05,485
the same package, every time.

280
00:16:06,033 --> 00:16:07,908
This is most of Helm at work:

281
00:16:07,908 --> 00:16:13,663
installing ingress controllers, monitoring, cert manager, from their maintainers' charts.

282
00:16:15,400 --> 00:16:20,463
podinfo, a small, well-maintained demo application with a public chart.

283
00:16:20,463 --> 00:16:23,029
Search shows chart and app versions.

284
00:16:23,567 --> 00:16:28,534
Before installing, render it and look at what it creates and which images it pulls.

285
00:16:28,534 --> 00:16:31,826
A Deployment and a Service with the maintainer's image.

286
00:16:31,826 --> 00:16:36,913
But also three test Pods with three more images from Docker Hub, one without any tag.

287
00:16:36,913 --> 00:16:39,486
That is the kind of detail a review is for.

288
00:16:41,233 --> 00:16:45,126
Installed with a pinned version and a reviewed values file:

289
00:16:45,126 --> 00:16:47,633
our message, two replicas, an Ingress.

290
00:16:48,167 --> 00:16:49,732
And the other direction:

291
00:16:49,732 --> 00:16:55,733
package demo app and push it to an O C I registry, here a local one standing in for G H C R.

292
00:16:56,267 --> 00:17:01,221
Anyone can now install it by reference and version, instead of copying a folder.

293
00:17:02,967 --> 00:17:07,766
The Bookshop chart depends on a small PostgreSQL chart from this repository.

294
00:17:08,300 --> 00:17:14,489
The repository can be a folder, an H T T P repository, or an O C I registry.

295
00:17:15,033 --> 00:17:17,083
And a condition makes it optional:

296
00:17:17,083 --> 00:17:21,183
the bundled database for dev and C I, an external one in production.

297
00:17:22,933 --> 00:17:26,569
A dependency must be in the parent's charts folder.

298
00:17:26,569 --> 00:17:28,138
Declared, but missing.

299
00:17:28,700 --> 00:17:32,607
helm dependency update downloads it and writes Chart.lock:

300
00:17:32,607 --> 00:17:34,292
exact version and digest.

301
00:17:34,292 --> 00:17:37,121
Commit the lock, and build from it in C I.

302
00:17:37,667 --> 00:17:43,336
One release, objects from two charts, and the child configured through the parent's values.

303
00:17:45,100 --> 00:17:50,370
Five services, the database from the dependency, and a completed report Job:

304
00:17:50,370 --> 00:17:54,392
seven workloads from two charts, one release, one command.

305
00:17:54,392 --> 00:17:56,264
Compare with chapter three.

306
00:17:56,800 --> 00:17:57,783
And it works:

307
00:17:57,783 --> 00:18:03,381
dev environment, users from PostgreSQL, with a password nobody ever typed.

308
00:18:05,133 --> 00:18:08,038
A hook is an ordinary Job with an annotation.

309
00:18:08,038 --> 00:18:11,071
Helm creates it at its moment and waits for it.

310
00:18:11,071 --> 00:18:17,203
Pre install before anything exists, post install after, pre and post upgrade around an upgrade.

311
00:18:17,767 --> 00:18:21,561
Pre delete runs on uninstall, before anything is deleted.

312
00:18:21,561 --> 00:18:22,293
And notice:

313
00:18:22,293 --> 00:18:25,954
all five hook Jobs are still there after the uninstall.

314
00:18:25,954 --> 00:18:28,351
They were never part of the release.

315
00:18:28,351 --> 00:18:31,413
Hooks are powerful, and they add moving parts.

316
00:18:33,167 --> 00:18:35,150
The Bookshop has exactly one hook:

317
00:18:35,150 --> 00:18:39,701
after every install and upgrade, the report worker writes a deployment report.

318
00:18:39,701 --> 00:18:43,201
A task that must run at a precise moment of each deployment:

319
00:18:43,201 --> 00:18:45,418
that is when a hook is the right tool.

320
00:18:45,967 --> 00:18:50,109
Its delete policy replaces the previous Job just before the next run,

321
00:18:50,109 --> 00:18:52,571
so the last report's log is always there.

322
00:18:54,333 --> 00:18:57,917
Now a pre upgrade hook fails, as a migration might.

323
00:18:57,917 --> 00:19:01,220
The upgrade fails immediately, naming the hook.

324
00:19:01,767 --> 00:19:06,095
Revision two is failed, and the ConfigMap still holds the old message:

325
00:19:06,095 --> 00:19:07,331
nothing was touched.

326
00:19:07,331 --> 00:19:09,743
That is what a pre upgrade hook is for.

327
00:19:09,743 --> 00:19:15,431
Before writing one, ask whether an init container, a normal Job or a pipeline step would do:

328
00:19:15,431 --> 00:19:17,966
a rollback does not undo what a hook did.

329
00:19:19,700 --> 00:19:22,747
A Helm test is a Pod that only helm test creates.

330
00:19:22,747 --> 00:19:26,168
The Bookshop's calls every service through its Service,

331
00:19:26,168 --> 00:19:32,386
the A P Is check their database connection, and the frontend must report this release's environment.

332
00:19:32,386 --> 00:19:33,506
All checks passed.

333
00:19:33,506 --> 00:19:36,366
Run it after every upgrade, and on a schedule.

334
00:19:38,100 --> 00:19:40,939
Someone scales java A P I to zero by hand.

335
00:19:40,939 --> 00:19:43,101
helm status still says deployed:

336
00:19:43,101 --> 00:19:47,156
Helm records what it applied, it does not watch the cluster.

337
00:19:47,700 --> 00:19:48,963
The test does.

338
00:19:48,963 --> 00:19:54,376
After go status, the next check, java A P I, cannot connect.

339
00:19:54,933 --> 00:19:57,836
Compare what Helm applied with what is live:

340
00:19:57,836 --> 00:20:01,200
the release says one replica, the cluster has zero.

341
00:20:01,200 --> 00:20:01,596
Drift.

342
00:20:01,596 --> 00:20:06,148
An upgrade re-applies the release's state, and the test passes again.

343
00:20:07,900 --> 00:20:12,606
Installing a chart creates Kubernetes resources, with your credentials.

344
00:20:12,606 --> 00:20:15,854
Traefik, from chapter one, created a ClusterRole:

345
00:20:15,854 --> 00:20:18,306
permissions across the whole cluster.

346
00:20:18,867 --> 00:20:20,179
Read what it may do:

347
00:20:20,179 --> 00:20:25,100
get, list and watch Secrets in every namespace, to load T L S certificates.

348
00:20:25,100 --> 00:20:30,086
Expected for an ingress controller, and worth knowing before you install it.

349
00:20:30,633 --> 00:20:32,404
And what the Bookshop runs:

350
00:20:32,404 --> 00:20:35,224
every Pod non root, no A P I token mounted.

351
00:20:35,224 --> 00:20:41,848
The audit found one gap while this course was being built, the database Pod, and the chart was fixed.

352
00:20:43,600 --> 00:20:45,467
Values files end up in Git.

353
00:20:45,467 --> 00:20:47,610
And dash dash set is not safer:

354
00:20:47,610 --> 00:20:50,515
the value is stored in the release record.

355
00:20:51,067 --> 00:20:54,563
Here it is, read back with nothing but kubectl:

356
00:20:54,563 --> 00:20:57,911
base sixty-four, base sixty-four again, gzip.

357
00:20:57,911 --> 00:20:59,771
Encoding, not encryption.

358
00:20:59,771 --> 00:21:04,011
Anyone who can read Secrets in the namespace can read it.

359
00:21:04,567 --> 00:21:05,636
The safe patterns:

360
00:21:05,636 --> 00:21:10,269
generate the password in the cluster, or reference an existing Secret by name.

361
00:21:10,269 --> 00:21:13,892
With existing secret set, the chart renders no Secret at all:

362
00:21:13,892 --> 00:21:19,713
every service and the database point at the named one, and the password never passes through Helm.

363
00:21:21,467 --> 00:21:24,709
Twelve broken deployments, and one method.

364
00:21:24,709 --> 00:21:25,712
Render first:

365
00:21:25,712 --> 00:21:27,565
is the chart even valid?

366
00:21:28,100 --> 00:21:28,808
Compare:

367
00:21:28,808 --> 00:21:31,994
is what Helm applied what you meant?

368
00:21:31,994 --> 00:21:34,295
Values, manifest, history.

369
00:21:34,833 --> 00:21:35,407
Observe:

370
00:21:35,407 --> 00:21:38,488
what does Kubernetes say about the objects?

371
00:21:39,033 --> 00:21:43,728
Fix through Helm, in values or the chart, never only in the cluster.

372
00:21:44,267 --> 00:21:47,691
And verify, with a test and the application itself.

373
00:21:47,691 --> 00:21:51,451
Three of the twelve, the ones that surprise most people.

374
00:21:53,200 --> 00:21:56,210
A tidy-up adds a label to the selector helper.

375
00:21:56,210 --> 00:21:57,388
The upgrade fails:

376
00:21:57,388 --> 00:21:59,810
a Deployment's selector is immutable.

377
00:22:00,367 --> 00:22:02,214
So nothing changed?

378
00:22:02,214 --> 00:22:03,866
The site is down.

379
00:22:04,400 --> 00:22:09,072
The Service accepted the new selector before the Deployment was rejected.

380
00:22:09,072 --> 00:22:13,168
Helm stops at the first error and does not undo what it applied.

381
00:22:13,168 --> 00:22:15,024
No Pod matches, no endpoints.

382
00:22:15,024 --> 00:22:17,840
A rollback restores one consistent revision.

383
00:22:19,600 --> 00:22:23,306
Someone patched a ConfigMap with kubectl during debugging.

384
00:22:23,306 --> 00:22:28,417
The test fails; the diff of Helm's manifest against the cluster shows the drift:

385
00:22:28,417 --> 00:22:29,695
prod instead of dev.

386
00:22:30,233 --> 00:22:31,650
The repair fails too.

387
00:22:31,650 --> 00:22:34,415
Helm four applies with server side apply,

388
00:22:34,415 --> 00:22:39,540
which records an owner for every field, and kubectl patch now owns this one.

389
00:22:39,540 --> 00:22:42,103
Helm refuses to overwrite it silently.

390
00:22:42,667 --> 00:22:47,441
We know the manual change was wrong, so Helm takes the field back:

391
00:22:47,441 --> 00:22:49,321
dash dash force conflicts.

392
00:22:49,321 --> 00:22:51,636
A safety net, used deliberately.

393
00:22:53,400 --> 00:22:59,746
Production, autoscaling, and a request for five replicas with dash dash set.

394
00:22:59,746 --> 00:23:01,166
Still three Pods.

395
00:23:01,166 --> 00:23:02,919
Did the value arrive?

396
00:23:02,919 --> 00:23:03,253
Yes:

397
00:23:03,253 --> 00:23:04,756
precedence worked.

398
00:23:05,300 --> 00:23:11,451
But the rendered Deployment has no replicas field at all, and an autoscaler with a minimum of three.

399
00:23:11,451 --> 00:23:15,018
The template ignores replica count when autoscaling is on.

400
00:23:15,018 --> 00:23:19,385
Precedence decides a key's value; the chart decides whether it matters.

401
00:23:19,385 --> 00:23:22,891
Scale through the autoscaler's minimum, in a values file.

402
00:23:24,633 --> 00:23:25,421
Lab fifteen:

403
00:23:25,421 --> 00:23:27,717
a release with more than one fault.

404
00:23:27,717 --> 00:23:32,442
The triage script prints Helm's view and Kubernetes' view in one screen.

405
00:23:32,442 --> 00:23:33,361
First anomaly:

406
00:23:33,361 --> 00:23:36,248
the image tag one point zero does not exist.

407
00:23:36,800 --> 00:23:38,490
Fix it, triage again:

408
00:23:38,490 --> 00:23:43,400
now the Pod runs, but its probes fail on port three thousand.

409
00:23:43,400 --> 00:23:46,862
A second fault was hiding behind the first.

410
00:23:46,862 --> 00:23:50,081
One hypothesis, one fix, verify, repeat.

411
00:23:51,833 --> 00:23:55,122
The production style chart reads like a list of answers.

412
00:23:55,122 --> 00:23:59,645
Five near-identical blocks become one template that ranges over the services.

413
00:24:00,200 --> 00:24:03,292
The environment folders become values files:

414
00:24:03,292 --> 00:24:05,612
three, nine and thirty-two lines.

415
00:24:06,167 --> 00:24:11,695
URLs that depend on the release are built with T P L; the database password is generated once,

416
00:24:11,695 --> 00:24:16,460
read back with lookup on every upgrade, and kept when the release is uninstalled,

417
00:24:16,460 --> 00:24:18,283
because the volume outlives it.

418
00:24:18,833 --> 00:24:21,670
The database is a dependency with a condition.

419
00:24:22,233 --> 00:24:25,293
A hook, a test and NOTES make it operable.

420
00:24:25,833 --> 00:24:29,323
And production adds an autoscaler and disruption budgets.

421
00:24:31,067 --> 00:24:32,229
Dev and staging:

422
00:24:32,229 --> 00:24:33,465
the same objects.

423
00:24:33,465 --> 00:24:37,753
Production adds the autoscaler and five disruption budgets.

424
00:24:38,300 --> 00:24:41,183
And the gate a promotion pipeline uses:

425
00:24:41,183 --> 00:24:43,401
the test of every environment.

426
00:24:43,401 --> 00:24:45,766
Succeeded, succeeded, succeeded.

427
00:24:47,500 --> 00:24:49,990
The capstone runs the whole life cycle.

428
00:24:49,990 --> 00:24:53,630
Deploy staging with raw YAML and measure the duplication.

429
00:24:54,167 --> 00:24:57,261
Build the chart, or study the reference one.

430
00:24:57,800 --> 00:25:01,983
Lint and render for every environment, install, verify.

431
00:25:02,533 --> 00:25:08,968
Upgrade node A P I to one point one point zero, through a small overlay file, reviewed as a diff.

432
00:25:09,533 --> 00:25:13,428
Break the next upgrade, investigate it, roll back.

433
00:25:13,967 --> 00:25:17,101
Test, read the history, clean up.

434
00:25:18,867 --> 00:25:20,757
The upgrade, previewed:

435
00:25:20,757 --> 00:25:23,386
only node A P I's image changes.

436
00:25:23,933 --> 00:25:27,062
Then a java A P I tag that was never published:

437
00:25:27,062 --> 00:25:30,058
the upgrade fails, the old Pod keeps serving.

438
00:25:30,600 --> 00:25:34,426
Roll back to revision two, and the test proves the state:

439
00:25:34,426 --> 00:25:39,930
java A P I back on one point zero, node A P I on one point one, all checks passed.

440
00:25:41,667 --> 00:25:43,413
Read the history as a story:

441
00:25:43,413 --> 00:25:48,837
installed, node A P I one point one, a failed java A P I promotion, rolled back to two.

442
00:25:48,837 --> 00:25:54,075
And production runs chart one point two point one, staging one point two point zero:

443
00:25:54,075 --> 00:25:58,065
the chart version tells you which package each environment runs.

444
00:25:58,065 --> 00:26:01,183
Nothing about this needs a meeting to reconstruct.

445
00:26:02,933 --> 00:26:06,524
Twelve challenges build one chart from an empty folder:

446
00:26:06,524 --> 00:26:09,527
values, environments, a ConfigMap, an Ingress,

447
00:26:09,527 --> 00:26:15,012
a helper, an upgrade, a rollback, a dependency, a test, and a broken release to fix.

448
00:26:15,567 --> 00:26:16,747
And the cleanup:

449
00:26:16,747 --> 00:26:20,806
uninstall, remove the repositories, delete the cluster.

450
00:26:22,567 --> 00:26:29,588
You can now explain why Helm exists, how charts, values and templates work, and how to manage releases.

451
00:26:30,133 --> 00:26:32,813
How to run many environments from one chart,

452
00:26:32,813 --> 00:26:37,197
use repositories and dependencies, and use hooks and tests deliberately.

453
00:26:37,733 --> 00:26:41,604
And how to review a chart's security, troubleshoot with a method,

454
00:26:41,604 --> 00:26:45,535
and operate a production style chart through its whole life cycle.

455
00:26:45,535 --> 00:26:47,739
Everything is in the free repository.

456
00:26:47,739 --> 00:26:48,930
Thanks for watching.
